A||||3||BN Boomer||What does this coolant pipe actually do?||||1||
Z||000000||BN Boomer||03-22-2001||01:02 PM||||I'm swapping an 88 2.8 into my 85 GT. On the original 2.8 in the GT, it has a small coolant pipe that exits the water pump at about the 10 o'clock position. It points towards the upper rear of the engine, connects to a hose that goes to the rear wall of the engine compartment where it connects to another coolant pipe the goes to the bottom of the engine compartment, turns to travel towards the front of the car, and disappears beneath the battery tray.<P>The 88 2.8 does not have this small pipe on the water pump. I'm assuming that whatever function this portion of the cooling system performs was deemed unnecessary and done away with in the 88 V6s. The hole for it is still there on the water pump, but it has a plug threaded into it. I can't get this plug out and was wondering if I could just put another plug in the coolant hose in back of the motor rather than swapping out the water pump right now.<P>Any thoughts or suggestions?||||reg||1
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||03-22-2001||01:23 PM||||The pipe in question goes down and connects to the passenger side coolant pipe just behind the door.  You can see it if you just look under the car and trace the pipe back....to get out the plug we have had to warm up the water pump with a torch (NOT TOO HOT!) but just enough if not I think that a machine shop would be able to help. You will need to have the pipe from your old 2.8 water pump to make things they way should be in an 85. <P>Now Im not sure because Ive never had reason to do it but I wonder if that pipe can't just be plugged off and you can continue your life...unfortunately, if that plug were to ever fail on you the coolant would literally be rooster tailing out of your Fiero!<P> Hope this helps!<p>[This message has been edited by WKDFIRO (edited 03-22-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||Gold-86SE||03-22-2001||02:04 PM||||<BR>Boomer,<P>To me, if I am understanding your description properly, regarding the exit pipe at the 10 o'clock postion on the water pump and routes to the pipe next to the strut tower is this:<P>The water pump sends water into this pipe which travels along the strut tower, then along the fire wall to the hose that runs toward the heater pipe, which runs on the passenger side of the fuel tank.  This may have been redirected in the 88, so you may have to determine how that is run in an 88 or just go with out the heater.<P>My $0.000000000002 cents.<P>------------------<BR>84 SE 4.10 4 Speed (Down and Out for the Count)<BR>85 GT Automatic (Restoration Project)<BR>86 SE 4 Speed (Fair Weather Daily Driver)<BR>87 SC 5 Speed (Not Running, V8 Conversion?)<BR>Please visit my <A HREF="http://www.trifocus.net/~fws/index.html" TARGET=_blank>Fiero Work Shop</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||BN Boomer||03-22-2001||02:16 PM||||Thanks for the quick responses, guys. These forums are the best. Now that I know what direction I need to go I'll get to it. I do still have the outlet pipe from the 85, so hopefully it's just a matter of getting that darn plug out. I guess I'll just have to work on that plug in the water pump a little bit more and if that doesn't work, I'll simply have to swap in a new pump. A new water pump never hurt anyone and they aren't very expensive. Thanks again.||||reg||1
